  "enhanced_information": "The 2-year-old boy's symptoms, including facial swelling, irritability, and red urine, suggest a post-streptococcal complication, likely acute rheumatic fever (ARF). The elevated antistreptolysin O indicates a recent streptococcal infection, while decreased serum C3 suggests complement consumption, consistent with ARF's autoimmune response. ARF is known to trigger autoimmune mechanisms similar to those seen in rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and polyarteritis nodosa (PAN), both of which are autoimmune diseases. Myasthenia gravis, while an autoimmune disorder, is not triggered by a similar mechanism as ARF. The Arthus reaction is a type III hypersensitivity reaction but does not share the same underlying mechanism as ARF. Therefore, the conditions triggered by a similar mechanism to ARF are RA and PAN. The best-supported option is III (acute rheumatic fever), IV (polyarteritis nodosa), and potentially V (rheumatoid arthritis), but the strongest consensus aligns with III and IV.",
